Colorectal Polyps
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Often asymptomatic/incidental, Intermittent rectal bleeding, Iron deficiency anaemia, Mucosal protrusion on colonoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-014
Reason for Visit: Recurrent, bright red rectal bleeding over several weeks causing severe patient anxiety and distress regarding potential cancer, now requesting urgent evaluation.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted recurrent rectal bleeding causing severe acute anxiety in a patient. While likely non-emergent (suspected hemorrhoids or polyp), an urgent after-hours assessment was required to perform a physical examination, exclude massive lower GI haemorrhage or a palpable low rectal mass, and provide immediate reassurance and appropriate referral to prevent inappropriate presentation to the Emergency Department.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I keep seeing bright red blood in the toilet bowl, and I am terrified I have bowel cancer."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports intermittent visible bright red rectal bleeding coating the stool and dripping into the toilet bowl over the past 3 weeks. Denies any severe abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. Reports mild fatigue but no overt shortness of breath. Notes a slight change in bowel habits with occasional mild constipation. Denies tenesmus or feeling a prolapsing mass. No unintentional weight loss. Patient is highly anxious.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but distressed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe health anxiety driving the consultation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears well, well-nourished, but visibly anxious. No signs of acute hypovolaemia.
Physical Exam: Conjunctivae are pink; no overt clinical signs of severe anaemia. Abdomen is soft, non-tender, non-distended, with normal bowel sounds. No palpable masses or hepatomegaly. Visual inspection of the perianal area is unremarkable. Digital rectal examination reveals normal sphincter tone, no palpable masses, no internal haemorrhoids palpable, and trace bright red blood on the examining finger. The presentation aligns with intermittent rectal bleeding and potential underlying iron deficiency anaemia; mucosal protrusion on colonoscopy is suspected pending referral.
Environmental Observations: Normal living environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Colorectal Polyps - Status: Stable (Symptomatic bleeding)
Differentials:

Internal Haemorrhoids - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of palpable engorged vessels on digital rectal examination and the duration of bleeding without typical prolapse symptoms, though they remain a highly common differential requiring endoscopic exclusion.

Colorectal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of palpable masses, weight loss, or severe colicky abdominal pain, though the patient's age and bleeding mandate a colonoscopy to definitively rule this out.

Diverticular Bleed - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The low volume and intermittent nature of the bright red blood, whereas diverticular bleeds typically present as sudden, large-volume, painless hematochezia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with recurrent, low-volume bright red rectal bleeding causing significant psychological distress. An urgent after-hours home visit was necessary to perform a clinical abdominal and digital rectal examination to definitively exclude gross pathology (e.g., palpable low rectal cancer, massive acute hemorrhage) and to de-escalate the patient's acute anxiety. Finding no acute surgical emergency or hemodynamic instability, the clinical picture strongly points toward a benign source such as a bleeding colorectal polyp or internal hemorrhoid. Deferring care would have likely resulted in the patient unnecessarily attending a crowded emergency department out of fear. Prompt outpatient referral for definitive endoscopic diagnosis is the appropriate clinical pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Generated a non-urgent pathology request (FBC, Iron studies, Ferritin) to quantify potential chronic blood loss. Provided an urgent referral to a Gastroenterologist for a diagnostic and therapeutic colonoscopy.
Positive Behavioral Change: Increase your daily dietary fiber by adding exactly two tablespoons of psyllium husk to your morning cereal, followed by a large glass of water. This creates a soft, bulky stool that passes easily, mechanically preventing the hard stool from scraping and bleeding over any fragile polyps or internal vessels.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to discuss blood results and track the gastroenterology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the bleeding changes from small drops to passing large clots or a full cup of blood, or if you feel dizzy, lightheaded, or short of breath, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A physical exam cannot see the inside of your entire colon; a colonoscopy is required to find the exact source of the bleeding and remove it.
Clinical Course: Polyps are small, usually benign growths that can bleed easily. Removing them during a colonoscopy prevents them from potentially turning into cancer years down the line.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your physical exam today did not show any large, dangerous masses. However, if you start feeling faint when you stand up or if the amount of blood in the toilet dramatically increases, you must seek emergency medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bright red blood suggests the bleeding is coming from the very lower part of your bowel. Small fleshy growths called polyps are highly vascular and bleed easily when stool rubs against them. The colonoscopy will use a camera to locate these growths and immediately snip them off.
Actionable Steps: Do not ignore the bleeding just because you feel reassured today. You must complete the blood tests tomorrow and strictly attend the colonoscopy appointment to ensure any pre-cancerous growths are removed before they cause serious problems.

SMS INFORMATION

Rectal bleeding assessed. No acute emergency. Blood test forms and Gastroenterologist referral provided for colonoscopy.

SMS EDUCATION

